ZERO 0.13 NATIVE KIT VERIFICATION

Both kits contain exactly 20 files: original Zero-built executables, five original
compiler/runtime companions, and README.ztext. No external test harness, foreign
runtime, third-party GUI library, user source, screenshot or temporary file is
included. Archives were created and verified by the original zero-archive tool.
README and archive manifests were created by the original zero-text tool.

Native executables and compiler apps match integration/build-release byte for
byte. Ezero runtime parts match the frozen original source. The two archives were
extracted into independent directories whose full paths contain spaces. Contents
match staging byte for byte; Linux owner-execute flags were restored correctly.
Own zero-sha256 results also agree with the independent host hash calculation.

PASS: relocated Linux Zero and Ezero CLI compile and run original hello examples.
PASS: relocated Windows Zero and Ezero CLI compile and run under Wine.
PASS: Linux and Windows HTML-reader outputs are identical for the same HTML input.
PASS: original Windows archive reader verifies and extracts its Windows kit under
Wine; all extracted bytes match the staged kit.

PASS: Linux editor with Zero and Ezero sources, and Windows editor under Wine with
Zero and Ezero sources. Each of these four runs exercised keyboard input, save,
F5 compilation, execution of the generated program, mouse positioning, editing,
save/reopen, missing-file preservation and the unsaved-close guard.
Each run also checked deterministic successful F5 rebuild and a syntax-error
build leaving the previously generated executable byte-identical.
After the tests, the extracted toolkit directories still match staging exactly.

The GUI tests used the native Linux X11 implementation and the native Windows
Win32 implementation under Wine. This is not a separate physical Windows,
Debian, Arch or native Wayland certification. No Drive publication was performed.

